      body2: "Second half, where people fall after already opening the calendar: «data came in better than forecast — so I buy that country's currency». That's a cheat sheet that used to live in the previous edition of this very chapter, and we removed it because we checked it. In our sample, the sign of the surprise predicts the sign of the 30-minute move in {{dir_hit_rate}}% of cases — over {{n_releases_dir}} observations, confidence interval {{dir_ci}}. That's indistinguishable from a coin flip.",
      body3: "The truth sits in between, and it's more useful than either extreme: the calendar doesn't predict direction, it predicts scale. It tells you WHEN the market will move. How hard — our own measurements say that's less clear-cut than commonly assumed too (§3.4). Which way — it never says, and anyone who claims otherwise either hasn't measured it or measured it and didn't publish.",
