      body: "«Colleague, the most common question after your first week with the calendar is: „it came in better than forecast, why did it drop?” I always give the same answer: because the market wasn't trading the number, it was trading the expectation, and the expectation had already been in the price for three days before the release.\n\nPicture this: all week the talk is that the data will be strong. Whoever wanted to buy on that — bought early. A strong number comes out, and there's no one left to buy, but plenty of people ready to take profit. Price falls on «good» news, and there's no conspiracy here, just the arithmetic of positioning.\n\nWhat I actually do with the calendar — and it's more boring than people expect. I look at it to know what NOT to do. Don't enter with a market order ten minutes before a high-impact release. Don't hold a tight stop through a release — it won't get taken out by the move, it'll get taken out by the widened spread. Don't be surprised when a position that sat calm for four hours covers its daily target in a minute, in either direction.\n\nFor me the calendar is a schedule of risks, not opportunities. Opportunities grow out of it too, but much later, and only once you've learned not to get caught in that first minute by accident.»",
