      body: "A trader holds a gold position with a 15-point stop — a normal stop for their timeframe, sized per chapter 3. They didn't check the calendar. A high-impact release hits.\n\nTwo factors land at once, and both are measurable. First: the median bar size at the moment of such a release, per our data, is {{release_median_move}} of the daily range — noticeably more than their stop. Second, and this is what decides it: the spread widens at the moment of publication — the market maker pulled its orders (chapter 4). The stop doesn't fill at the stop price, it fills at the first available one.\n\nOutcome: the position closes not because the trader's analysis was wrong, but because they happened to be in the market at the exact moment the book was empty. Forty minutes later, price is back where it was. They conclude «my stop was too tight» and next time set it wider — increasing risk while solving the wrong problem.\n\nThe honest second half: this doesn't mean you can never hold a position through a release. It means the decision to hold must be a conscious one: either the position closes before the release, or its size is reduced ahead of time, or the stop is set knowing that in the first minute it guarantees nothing. All three are decisions. Not knowing about the release isn't one.",
